          <a href="#hydrolysate-blends" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Hydrolysates and Blends</a>
          <a href="#label" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Reading the Label</a>
          <a href="#who-should-buy" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Who Should Buy Which</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Whey isolate is not better protein than whey concentrate. At the same dose of protein, the two build the same muscle. Isolate is filtered further so that each scoop carries less lactose, carbohydrate and fat, and you pay for that filtering. If lactose bothers you or every gram of your diet is counted, it is worth it. If not, concentrate does the job for less.
        </p>

        <div id="processing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How Each One Is Made: Microfiltration vs Ion Exchange
          </h2>

          <p>
            Both products start in the same place. When milk is curdled to make cheese, the curds become cheese and the liquid left behind is whey. Fresh liquid whey is mostly water, with lactose, minerals and a small amount of fat, and its protein content is well under 1 percent. Turning that liquid into a protein powder is a matter of removing as much of everything else as possible, and the further a manufacturer takes that process, the purer the protein becomes.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whey protein concentrate is made by ultrafiltration, which passes the liquid across membranes that let water, lactose and minerals through while holding back the larger protein molecules. The retained liquid is then spray-dried into powder. Depending on how long the filtration runs, concentrate can be anywhere from about 34 to 89 percent protein by weight. The concentrate used in most sports powders is labeled WPC80 and sits close to 80 percent protein, with the remainder made up of lactose, fat, minerals and moisture.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whey protein isolate goes further, to at least 90 percent protein. The most common route is cross-flow microfiltration, in which whey is pumped along ceramic membranes at low temperatures so that fat and remaining lactose can be separated out without heating the protein enough to damage it. The other route is ion exchange, which passes whey through a column of charged resin that binds the proteins by their electrical charge, then releases them in a separate step. Both produce a genuine isolate.
          </p>

          <p>
            The two isolate methods do differ in detail. Ion exchange can achieve slightly higher protein percentages but tends to strip out more of the minor protein fractions found in whey, such as glycomacropeptide and lactoferrin. Microfiltration keeps more of them, which is why many brands advertise it. These fractions are interesting to researchers, but there is no good evidence that the amounts in a protein shake make a practical difference to muscle, recovery or immunity. Both methods give you an excellent protein source.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE KEY DISTINCTION</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Concentrate is roughly 80 percent protein. Isolate is at least 90 percent.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">The remaining 10 to 20 percent is what separates them: lactose, fat, minerals and moisture. The protein itself is essentially the same, with the same amino acids in nearly the same proportions.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="protein-lactose-fat" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Protein Percentage, Lactose and Fat
          </h2>

          <p>
            On a nutrition label, the extra filtering shows up as a few grams here and there. A typical isolate scoop delivers around 25 g of protein in roughly 100 to 120 calories, with a gram or less of fat and a gram or two of carbohydrate, depending on flavor. A typical concentrate scoop delivering a similar amount of protein runs a little higher in calories, with a couple of grams of fat and a few grams of carbohydrate, much of it lactose. These are general ranges; individual products vary, and flavoring systems add carbohydrate of their own.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Per scoop, roughly</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Whey concentrate (WPC80)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Whey isolate</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Protein by weight</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 70 to 80 percent</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">90 percent or more</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Lactose</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">One to a few grams</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Usually a fraction of a gram</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Fat</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 1.5 to 3 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 0.5 to 1 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Carbohydrate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 3 to 5 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 0 to 2 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Leucine per 25 g protein</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 2.5 to 3 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 2.5 to 3 g</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Lactose is the difference that matters most to most people. A cup of regular milk contains roughly 12 g of lactose. A scoop of concentrate-based powder usually contains somewhere from one to a few grams, and an isolate scoop usually contains well under one. Some isolates are marketed as lactose-free, though for most products the accurate description is very low in lactose rather than none at all.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fat is the other difference, and it is small. The fat in concentrate is milk fat, along with some phospholipids that come through filtration, and a couple of grams per scoop adds perhaps 15 to 25 calories. That is irrelevant to most people and only meaningful to those tracking macros to the gram, such as physique competitors preparing for a show.
          </p>

          <p>
            What does not change is the amino acid profile. Because both products are made from the same whey proteins, a 25 g dose of protein from either delivers essentially the same essential amino acids and roughly 2.5 to 3 g of leucine, the amino acid most closely associated with triggering muscle protein synthesis. That single fact explains why the muscle-building research comes out the way it does.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Cost per 25 g of Protein
          </h2>

          <p>
            Isolate costs more to make because the extra filtration takes time, equipment and yield. Every step that removes lactose and fat also loses some protein, so producing a kilogram of isolate requires more raw whey than producing a kilogram of concentrate. That cost is passed on, and at typical retail prices an isolate usually costs noticeably more per gram of protein than a concentrate or a concentrate-heavy blend from a comparable brand.
          </p>

          <p>
            The fair way to compare is cost per 25 g of protein, not price per tub or per serving. Divide the tub price by the number of servings, divide that by the grams of protein per serving, then multiply by 25. Scoop sizes vary widely, from about 20 g of protein to nearly 30 g, so two products advertising the same number of servings can deliver very different amounts of protein. Working out the cost per 25 g removes that distortion.
          </p>

          <p>
            As a worked example with round numbers rather than real prices: a concentrate costing 45 dollars for 30 servings of 24 g works out to about 1.56 dollars per 25 g of protein. An isolate costing 60 dollars for 30 servings of 25 g works out to 2 dollars per 25 g. In that example the isolate is about 28 percent more expensive for the same protein. Your numbers will differ, but gaps of that general size are common.
          </p>

          <p>
            Over a year, the difference adds up. Someone taking one scoop a day uses around 365 servings, so even a modest premium per serving becomes a meaningful sum. That money is well spent if you need what isolate provides. If you do not, it buys nothing you can measure, and the savings could go toward food, which supplies most of your protein anyway.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="digestion" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Digestion and the Lactose Question
          </h2>

          <p>
            Both forms of whey are fast-digesting proteins. They empty from the stomach quickly and raise blood amino acid levels within an hour or so, which is a large part of why whey is so widely studied. Isolate may be absorbed marginally faster because it carries less fat, which slows gastric emptying, but the difference is small next to the gap between whey and slow-digesting proteins such as casein.
          </p>

          <p>
            For most people, the digestion question is really a lactose question. Reduced lactase activity after childhood is common worldwide, affecting roughly two-thirds of adults by most estimates, though rates vary greatly by ancestry. Not everyone with low lactase gets symptoms, and dose is the deciding factor. A systematic review prepared for a US National Institutes of Health consensus conference concluded that most people with lactose intolerance can handle around 12 g of lactose in a single sitting with little or no discomfort.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is good news for both products. A scoop of concentrate usually sits well below that 12 g threshold, so many lactose-intolerant people tolerate it, especially mixed with water. Isolate goes further and removes most of the remaining lactose, which helps people who react to small amounts, such as some people with irritable bowel syndrome, and people taking several scoops a day. If you mix either powder into a glass of regular milk, the milk will be the main source of lactose, so use water or a lactose-free milk if this is your concern.
          </p>

          <p>
            Digestive trouble from a protein shake is not always about lactose. Sweeteners such as sugar alcohols, added fibers and gums can all cause bloating in sensitive people, and those ingredients appear in isolates as well as concentrates. If switching to isolate does not help, look at the rest of the ingredient list before blaming the whey itself. Anyone with persistent symptoms should talk to a doctor rather than troubleshooting by supplement.
          </p>

          <p>
            A milk protein allergy is a separate issue entirely. Allergies are immune reactions to the proteins themselves, and beta-lactoglobulin, the main whey protein, is a common trigger. Isolate contains just as much of it per gram of protein as concentrate does, so neither is safe for someone with a diagnosed milk allergy.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="muscle" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Muscle-Building Outcomes: No Meaningful Difference at Matched Protein
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the part of the comparison that marketing tends to blur. When researchers give people the same amount of protein from whey concentrate or whey isolate, the muscle protein synthesis response and the long-term gains in muscle and strength are essentially the same. There is no consistent evidence that the extra purity of isolate translates into more muscle. Both are complete proteins with high leucine content and fast digestion, and those are the properties that drive results.
          </p>

          <p>
            What does make a difference is how much protein you eat in total. The US Recommended Dietary Allowance is 0.8 g per kilogram of body weight per day, a floor for avoiding deficiency rather than a target for athletes. The International Society of Sports Nutrition position stand recommends 1.4 to 2.0 g/kg per day for people who exercise. A 2018 meta-analysis led by Robert Morton found that the benefit of extra protein for muscle gain plateaued at around 1.6 g/kg per day, with the upper end of the confidence interval near 2.2 g/kg. Our guide on <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">how much protein you need</a> turns those figures into daily targets.
          </p>

          <p>
            Per meal, research generally supports around 0.4 g/kg, or 20 to 40 g of protein, spread across three to five meals. One scoop of either concentrate or isolate typically delivers 20 to 28 g, which lands at the lower end of that range. Older adults appear to need larger doses for the same response, so two scoops can make sense for them. The source matters far less than hitting the dose.
          </p>

          <p>
            Timing follows the same pattern. A 2013 meta-analysis by Brad Schoenfeld, Alan Aragon and James Krieger found that the apparent benefit of taking protein right around a workout largely disappeared once total daily protein was accounted for. Isolate's slightly faster digestion therefore has no practical timing advantage. Take whichever powder you use at a time that helps you reach your daily target; our guide on <a href="/when-to-take-protein-powder/">when to take protein powder</a> covers the cases where timing matters a little more.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Match the protein dose and the results match too.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Isolate and concentrate build the same muscle at the same grams of protein. Spend your attention on total daily intake and per-meal dose, and choose between the two on lactose, macros and cost.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="hydrolysate-blends" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Hydrolyzed Whey and Isolate-Concentrate Blends
          </h2>

          <p>
            Hydrolyzed whey, or whey hydrolysate, is a third form that sits alongside concentrate and isolate. It is made by treating whey, usually isolate, with enzymes that cut the protein chains into shorter peptides, a partial pre-digestion. Hydrolysates tend to raise blood amino acid levels faster than intact whey, which is the basis for their premium pricing.
          </p>

          <p>
            The research has not found that faster absorption translates into more muscle. Studies comparing hydrolyzed and intact whey at matched protein doses generally report similar muscle protein synthesis and similar training outcomes. Whey is already fast, so there is little room for improvement. Hydrolysis also makes whey more bitter, and most labels do not disclose how far the hydrolysis was taken. Some buyers report that hydrolyzed products sit better in their stomach, which is a reasonable personal reason to choose one, but it is not a performance upgrade.
          </p>

          <p>
            One point is worth stressing. The partially hydrolyzed whey in sports powders is not the same as the extensively hydrolyzed formulas used for infants with cow milk allergy, and it should not be treated as safe for anyone with a milk allergy.
          </p>

          <p>
            Blends are the most common format on store shelves. Many of the best-selling whey powders combine isolate with concentrate, and sometimes with a little hydrolysate, to balance cost, texture and macros. A blend usually lands between the two pure forms on lactose, fat and price. For someone with no lactose sensitivity, a well-made blend is often the sweet spot: better macros than straight concentrate at a lower cost than straight isolate.
          </p>

          <p>
            The catch with blends is marketing. Front labels often feature the word isolate in large type when isolate is a minor ingredient. The ingredient list is the only reliable guide, which is the subject of the next section.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How to Tell What You Are Buying From the Label
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with the ingredient list, not the front of the tub. Ingredients are listed in descending order by weight, so the first protein named is the main protein source. A product listing whey protein isolate first and nothing else as a protein source is an isolate. A product listing whey protein concentrate first, followed by isolate, is a blend that is mostly concentrate, however prominently the word isolate appears on the front.
          </p>

          <p>
            Next, do some quick arithmetic. Divide the grams of protein per serving by the serving size in grams. A result close to 0.8 or above for an unflavored product, or somewhat lower for a flavored one because flavoring and sweetener take up weight, is consistent with a high-protein powder. A product claiming to be pure isolate whose protein is well under 70 percent of the scoop weight, with no obvious fillers, deserves a closer look. Carbohydrate and fat figures are useful too: an isolate should show very little of either.
          </p>

          <p>
            Watch for proprietary blends, which list ingredients under a brand name without disclosing amounts. They make it impossible to know how much of the protein comes from isolate. Also watch for free amino acids such as glycine, taurine or added creatine near the top of the list. Because protein content is commonly estimated from nitrogen, adding cheap nitrogen-rich amino acids can inflate the protein figure, a practice known as amino spiking. A clean isolate does not need them.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, check for independent certification. NSF Certified for Sport, Informed Sport and Informed Choice confirm that products have been tested for banned substances, and NSF also verifies that label claims match contents. Confirm the exact product and flavor in the certifier's own database, since listings change. Our guide to whether <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">protein powder is safe</a> covers contaminants and what each seal does and does not check.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Who Should Buy Which
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buy whey isolate if lactose causes you symptoms even in small amounts, if you take two or more scoops a day, if you are tracking macros tightly during a diet or show preparation, or if the certified product you want for a drug-tested sport happens to be an isolate. In those cases the premium buys something real. Our <a href="/best-whey-protein-isolate/">best whey protein isolate</a> rankings cover the options that hold up on label analysis and certification.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buy whey concentrate or a blend if you digest dairy comfortably, take one scoop a day and care more about cost than about a couple of grams of carbohydrate. Concentrate is also widely reported by buyers to taste creamier, and it is the better value for baking, smoothies made with milk and anything else where the extra filtering of isolate gets undone. Our <a href="/best-protein-powder/">best protein powder</a> guide includes strong concentrate and blend options.
          </p>

          <p>
            Consider plant protein instead if you have a milk protein allergy, follow a vegan diet or find that even isolate causes symptoms. Plant proteins build muscle well at adequate doses, though most contain less leucine per gram, so a slightly larger serving often helps. Our <a href="/whey-vs-plant-protein/">whey vs plant protein</a> comparison explains the tradeoffs.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whichever you choose, the same rules apply. Compare products on cost per 25 g of protein, read the ingredient list rather than the front label, look for independent certification, and size your intake to your actual daily target. People with kidney disease, those who are pregnant and anyone on medication should check with a doctor or registered dietitian before adding a protein supplement.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying for isolate you do not need</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                If you digest dairy comfortably and take one scoop a day, isolate's lower lactose and fat give you nothing you can measure. The muscle-building result at the same protein dose is the same as concentrate. Spending the premium is not harmful, but it is money that could go toward food that supplies most of your protein.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting the front of the tub</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Many blends print isolate prominently on the front while listing concentrate first in the ingredients. Since ingredients are listed by weight, the first protein named is the main one. Read the ingredient list before paying isolate prices, and be wary of proprietary blends that hide the proportions entirely.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mixing isolate into regular milk</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A cup of milk carries roughly 12 g of lactose, which is far more than the gram or two you saved by choosing isolate over concentrate. If lactose is your reason for buying isolate, mix it with water or lactose-free milk. Otherwise the premium you paid is cancelled out in the shaker.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming isolate is safe for a milk allergy</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Isolate removes most of the lactose but none of the milk proteins that cause allergic reactions. Beta-lactoglobulin, a common milk allergen, is the main protein in whey. Anyone with a diagnosed milk allergy should avoid both concentrate and isolate and ask an allergist about safe alternatives, usually a plant protein.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing price per serving</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Scoop sizes range from about 20 g of protein to nearly 30 g, so price per serving is not a fair comparison between brands or between concentrate and isolate. Calculate cost per 25 g of protein instead. It takes a minute and regularly shows that the cheaper-looking tub is the more expensive protein.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is whey isolate better than whey concentrate?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not for results. At the same dose of protein, the two produce essentially the same muscle protein synthesis and the same long-term gains because their amino acid profiles are nearly identical. Isolate is better only in what it leaves out: less lactose, fat and carbohydrate per scoop. That is worth paying for if you are lactose sensitive or tracking macros closely.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which is better for lactose intolerance, whey or whey isolate?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Isolate, because it removes most of the remaining lactose and usually contains a fraction of a gram per scoop. Many lactose-intolerant people also tolerate concentrate, since a scoop typically holds well under the roughly 12 g most can handle at once. If concentrate gives you symptoms, switch to isolate and mix it with water.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is whey isolate better for weight loss?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Slightly, in a narrow sense. Isolate has fewer calories per gram of protein, so it fits a tight calorie budget a little better. The difference is usually 10 to 30 calories per scoop, which is small. What matters for fat loss is the overall calorie deficit and enough protein to preserve muscle, which either form can provide.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does whey isolate absorb faster than concentrate?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Marginally. Concentrate carries a little more fat, which can slightly slow stomach emptying, so isolate may be absorbed a little faster. Both are fast-digesting proteins, and research has not shown that the small difference changes muscle gain. Total daily protein and per-meal dose matter far more than the minutes saved.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between whey isolate and hydrolyzed whey?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Isolate is whey filtered to at least 90 percent protein. Hydrolyzed whey has also been treated with enzymes that break the protein into shorter peptides, a partial pre-digestion. Hydrolysates absorb faster and cost more, but studies at matched protein doses have not found meaningfully better muscle gains. Many hydrolyzed products are made from isolate.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I mix whey concentrate and isolate?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Many popular powders are already blends of the two, and there is no problem combining them yourself. A blend usually lands between the two on lactose, fat and price. Some people use concentrate for baking and smoothies, where isolate's advantages disappear, and keep isolate for shakes mixed with water.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is whey concentrate lower quality than isolate?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Quality in the sense of amino acid content, digestibility and leucine is essentially the same, and both score at the top of protein quality measures. Concentrate simply contains more of the lactose, fat and minerals that naturally occur in whey. Product quality depends far more on the manufacturer, testing and formulation than on concentrate versus isolate.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

