        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Whey isolate and whey concentrate are the same protein at different levels of purity. Isolate is filtered to at least 90 percent protein, removing most of the lactose and fat, while concentrate sits around 80 percent. At matched protein doses they build the same muscle, so the choice comes down to lactose tolerance, how tightly you track macros and how much you are willing to pay per 25 g of protein.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            If lactose bothers you, you take several scoops a day or you need a specific certified product, buy an isolate. If none of that applies, a good concentrate or blend is the better value. Either way, read the ingredient list rather than the front label, compare on cost per 25 g of protein and check certifications in the certifier's own database.
          </p>
